# Changelog

All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

## [unreleased]

### Added

- Extend the Extension API with a `stmt_ext_atomicity` hook, declaring how much of the single atomic step allowed while an invariant is unfolded or an atomic update is in flight one of the extension's statements consumes: `NoStep` (ghost), `AtomicStep`, or `NonAtomicStep` (not permitted there at all). One function covers both statement extension points. The atomicity analysis cannot work this out for itself, since it runs before an extension statement is lowered and the lowering is generally several statements even for one indivisible step (see the `cas` case below). The base of the chain answers `NonAtomicStep`, so an unimplemented hook bars the statement from atomic blocks rather than waving it through as free. See `docs/ext/README.md`.
- Add `Library.Auth.full` to specify auth permissions that hold both the auth fragment and its full fragmental counterpart.
- Add a `--manifest` flag, printing one line of JSON describing what the binary is and what a client can expect of it: `version`, `lsp_protocol`, and `min_z3`. `lsp_protocol` versions the machine-readable interface an editor integration drives raven through -- the JSON diagnostic schema of `--lsp-mode` together with the flags such a client relies on -- separately from the release, and changes only when an existing client would notice the difference. The same JSON is published as a `manifest.json` release asset, alongside a `SHA256SUMS` covering the platform archives, so a client can tell whether it can drive a given release before downloading it, and verify what it got. This is what lets the VS Code extension pick up a new verifier on its own rather than having to be re-released to carry one.
- Substantially rework the tutorial: new content throughout, cram tests covering every code sample so the docs can no longer drift silently out of sync with the verifier, and a symbolic, auto-numbered section/cross-reference system for the tutorial site in place of hand-maintained numbering.

### Changed

- `Library.SetRA`'s data type now names its constructor and destructor `set`/`value` and its top case `top`, matching `Library.DisjSet`, instead of `set_constr`/`set_proj1`/`set_top`.
- The atomicity analysis's `Unclosed AU token or invariant` error -- raised when a body ends with an invariant still unfolded or an atomic update still open -- now names what was left open, `Missing fold for unfolded invariant i(x)` or `Missing commitAU or abortAU for open atomic update phi`, and is reported at the body's closing brace, where the omission is actually detected, rather than spanning the whole body. The `unfold`/`openAU` that opened it is given as a Related Location, as is anything else left open, so a body with several omissions names them all in one run.
- Identifiers in user-facing messages that quote an expression now print as written in the source, without the numbers the disambiguation pass gives a callable's locals: `Atomic token phi is already open` rather than `Atomic token phi^1 is already open`, and likewise for the messages above and the type-argument-inference, inhale/exhale and atomic-primitive ones. Internal errors are unchanged -- there, telling two same-named variables apart is the point.
- Rework the prophecy extension's `Proph` type and allocation/resolution syntax. One-shot prophecies now carry their own dedicated resource instead of reusing the multi-shot one, so resolving a one-shot prophecy a second time is a verification error rather than silently accepted.
- Scope backend SMT declarations hierarchically by module instead of declaring everything reachable from the program (library included) into one flat, never-popped context. A symbol is now only in scope for the proof obligations that actually need it, cutting down how much unrelated background axiom load a given check carries and making individual proofs less exposed to unrelated growth elsewhere in the program.


### Fixed

- Fix the atomicity analysis ignoring extension statements entirely, so that any number of them could be taken while an invariant was open or an atomic update in flight: `unfold inv(c); x := faa(c.f, 1); y := faa(c.g, 1); fold inv(c)` was accepted, as were `cas`, `xchg`, `cmpxchg` and the `--extension eris` samplers. Such a statement is still an unlowered extension tag when the analysis runs, and had no case, so it fell through to a no-op catch-all. The active extension now declares each statement's cost through the new `stmt_ext_atomicity` hook above: `cas`/`faa`/`xchg`/`cmpxchg` and the `eris` samplers cost one atomic step, while prophecy `newProph`/`resolve`, `EC.contra()` and `assert ... with { ... }` are ghost and cost nothing (a prophecy resolution in particular has to be able to sit alongside the physical step it is attached to). Extension statements now also invalidate mask entries mentioning the variables they assign, as ordinary assignments already did.
- Fix source files with CRLF line endings failing to lex at all: the carriage return matched no rule and was reported as `Syntax Error: Unexpected character` at the end of the first line. The lexer now accepts `\r\n` wherever it accepts `\n`, and treats the carriage return as part of the line break rather than of the line's text, so reported line and column numbers are identical to those for the same file with LF endings, and a CRLF break ends a statement (for automatic semicolon insertion) just as an LF one does. A stray carriage return that is not part of a line break remains a lexical error.
- Fix a statement terminated by an inferred rather than a written semicolon being recorded as ending at the *next* statement's first token, so a diagnostic reported against the statement as a whole underlined into the following line (and, under `--lsp-mode`, made the editor's squiggle swallow the next line's leading keyword). Deciding whether a line break ends a statement needs one token of lookahead, and lexing it left the lexer's positions describing that lookahead token rather than the line break; the inserted semicolon now carries the line break's own position, and the lookahead is replayed with the positions it was lexed at. Writing the semicolon explicitly was the workaround.
- Fix statements beginning with an optional `ghost` modifier (`var`/`val` definitions) being recorded at a location starting at the end of the *preceding* token, so a diagnostic against the statement as a whole underlined from the end of the previous line. The modifier came from a nullable grammar rule, whose start position menhir resolves that way when it matches nothing.
- Fix the atomicity analysis rejecting a conditional that occurs while an invariant is open or an atomic update is in flight, even when each arm takes only a single atomic step -- e.g. a distributed counter whose `incr` bumps one of two backing fields depending on a local flag. The join was treated as a non-atomic step, which is unconditionally forbidden inside an atomic block, so the program had to pay for the conditional itself. It shouldn't: heap reads are not permitted in a condition, so evaluating the test only inspects thread-local state, and only one arm ever runs. The join now carries forward the disjunction of the two arms' atomic-step flags, so a conditional costs whatever its more expensive arm costs. An arm taking two atomic steps, or a further step after the join, is still rejected as before.
- Fix two misleading errors around a functor used as a type argument, e.g. `Auth[Set[Proph]]` where `Auth`'s parameter requires `ResourceAlgebra` (more than a representation type) and `Set` supplies only a type: this previously failed later, deep in module elaboration, naming an internal synthesized module (`Module TypeMod$$...$$Set''TypeExt'' must be declared as an interface. The value id is still abstract.`) instead of the actual mismatch. It's now rejected immediately, naming the type, the required interface, and which operations (`id`, `comp`, ...) it's missing. Separately, an identifier that fails to resolve at all inside `M[T1,...,Tn]` (e.g. a functor argument used without its `import`) was reported as `A functor can only be instantiated as the definition of a module ..., not used as a type or value here` instead of `Unknown identifier`, since both cases collapsed to the same internal `None`; the unknown-identifier case is now distinguished and reported correctly.
- Fix two lexer issues with automatic semicolon insertion: a bare `return` (no return values) directly followed by a newline no longer needs an explicit trailing `;` to parse; and a `,` immediately after an operator character with no space (e.g. `AtomicToken<P>,`) no longer merges with it into an unrecognized multi-character operator.
- Fix the backend crashing with an internal Z3 parse error on a data type's constructor/destructor when instantiated over a generic whose SMT-mangled name contains a stray `'` (e.g. a resource algebra like `SetRA` instantiated over a bracketed type argument). Such identifiers were printed unsanitized, unlike plain variable references, which already had their `'`s replaced.


## [1.2.0] - 2026-08-01

### Added

- Semicolons after statements in procedure/lemma bodies are now optional: the lexer performs automatic semicolon insertion based on line breaks, tracking bracket/parenthesis nesting and trailing operators so that only newlines that could plausibly end a statement are turned into semicolons. Semicolons remain otherwise unsupported outside of statement lists and data type case branches.
- Functors whose formals are all constrained by a rep-typed module/interface (e.g. `Library.Type`) can now be used without an explicit instantiation: `M[Int]` is accepted directly as a type, and calls like `M.foo(3)` are resolved by inferring the missing type argument(s) from the call's arguments and expected type, synthesizing (and deduplicating) the instantiation on demand.
- Explicit module instantiation statements accept the same rep-typed sugar: `module M = F[Int]` now auto-wraps a bare type argument (for any formal constrained by a rep-typed interface) into a synthesized module implementing that interface, deduplicated the same way as the type-position case above, instead of requiring the caller to hand-write the wrapper module (e.g. `module IntType : Library.Type { rep type T = Int }`) and pass it by name.
- A subexpression can now be annotated with a type, `(e: T)`, or, as a call argument, `f(e: T)` without the extra parens the standalone form would need. The annotation is checked, not taken for granted: `e` is checked against `T`, and `T` is checked against the surrounding expected type, using the same consistency check as everywhere else. This gives a way to disambiguate an otherwise-underdetermined expression, e.g. an empty collection literal like `{||}` passed to an uninstantiated generic functor: `M.set({||}: Set[Int])`.
- Add a `decreases` contract clause for checking termination of recursive `func`s, `proc`s, `lemma`s, and `while` loops, implemented as a new always-on extension (`lib/ext/decreasesExt/`, independent of `--extension`). `decreases e1, ..., en` declares a lexicographic termination measure whose components may be of any type with a `WellFoundedOrder` instance, resolved automatically from each component's own type (`lib/ext/decreasesExt/well_founded_order.rav` ships `IntOrder`, `Ordinal`, `LexOrder`, and `MultisetOrder`; every instance embeds into the ordinals and proves its order agrees with that embedding, reducing the whole scheme's soundness to the well-foundedness of the ordinal encoding itself) -- a self-recursive `data` type with no instance in scope gets one auto-generated instead, trusted rather than proven well-founded. A check that the measure strictly decreases according to the resolved order is inserted before every recursive call. Checking is opt-in per callable: a recursive callable with no `decreases` clause is left unchecked, exactly as before this extension existed. Mutually-recursive callables are supported too: a call between two *different* callables is checked whenever they lie in the same strongly-connected component of the module's call graph, not just literal self-calls. Every member of such a group must declare a `decreases` clause of the same lexicographic arity using the same `WellFoundedOrder` instance at each position, or checking that group is a type error -- partial or inconsistent coverage would give a false sense of a checked termination guarantee for the whole cycle.
- Generalize the Extension API with a fourth extension point, `contract_ext`, alongside the existing `type_ext`/`expr_ext`/`stmt_ext`: extensions can now attach their own clauses to callable and loop contracts (new `call_decl_contract_ext`/`loop_contract_ext` fields) instead of being limited to new types/expressions/statements. Adds matching `ExtApi.Ext` hooks for type-checking, printing, and rewriting contract-clause extensions (`type_check_contract_ext`, `contract_ext_to_string`, `contract_ext_rewrite_exprs`), plus two new kinds of hook with no precedent among the other three extension points, since a contract clause is a property of an entire callable/loop rather than one AST node to rewrite in isolation: `rewrite_callable_entry` (runs once per callable before its body is otherwise visited, e.g. to declare and initialize ghost locals) and `rewrite_contract_ext_call` (runs at call sites whose callee declares a contract-clause extension, to insert instrumentation such as the `decreases` progress check). The `decreases` extension above is the reference implementation; see `docs/ext/README.md` for the full API.
- Invariants can now have multiple distinct instances open at once, e.g. `unfold node(prev); unfold node(cur);` for a `prev != cur` provable at that point -- needed for patterns like hand-over-hand (lock-coupling) traversal. Previously rejected unconditionally: the mask tracking which invariants are available to open was keyed purely by declaration name, with no notion of instance identity, so opening any instance consumed the invariant's only slot. The mask model is now hierarchical, Iris-namespace-style: an invariant's identity is its own argument list, and a mask entry can specify any prefix of it (the whole declaration, one exact instance, or anything in between); opening two instances of the same invariant requires proving them disjoint, an ordinary SMT-discharged assertion generated automatically at the `unfold`. The mask a callable requires from its own caller is inferred from its own `requires` clause (kept in sync with an interface declaration and its implementation by construction, since those already have to match exactly) together with whatever its transitively-called callees require; a callable that allocates and immediately reopens its own invariant instance -- including sharing that access with a `spawn`ed thread -- needs nothing from its own caller for it, since folding grants mask credit path-sensitively through the callable's own control flow. Programs that never hold more than one instance of a given invariant open at a time are unaffected.
- `assert e with { ... }` now also supports `exists`-headed goals: the proof block computes a concrete witness (via assignment to the bound variable) instead of proving the body for an arbitrary instance.
- Add a `--strict` command-line flag that warns about recursive `func`s/`lemma`s (including loops inside a `lemma`) with no `decreases` clause, and about explicit uses of `free`. These are already silently assumed rather than checked regardless of `--strict`; the flag just surfaces a warning for them. Off by default.
- Add `--dump-library DIR` and `--print-library-source PATH`, which write out the library sources embedded in the binary (the standard library plus the active extension's) either all at once, reproducing the paths they are reported under, or one at a time to stdout. Both produce exactly the bytes the binary verifies against. Locations inside a library source name no file on disk, so an editor previously had nothing to open when a diagnostic pointed at one; it can now display the source instead. Library sources are also now identified by their path within the repository (`lib/library/resource_algebra.rav` rather than `resource_algebra.rav`), which appears in error messages, keeps two extensions from shipping colliding basenames, and gives `--dump-library` a sensible tree.
- `--lsp-mode` now marks a location inside a library source with `"library": true`, and adds `"path"` with the real file when the binary is running inside a checkout whose copy of that file is byte-identical to the embedded one. An editor can then open the actual source where there is one and fall back to serving the embedded text read-only where there isn't. Comparing content is what makes the path safe to follow: a checkout at a different revision, or a library file edited since the build, is rejected rather than reported with line numbers that no longer match what was verified.
- Add a from-scratch tutorial, covering sequential Raven, ownership, the module system, ghost code and basic concurrency, and a set of advanced capstones (fork/join, atomic contracts, iterated separating conjunctions), with every code listing a real, checked `.rav` file. Published at https://nyu-acsys.github.io/raven/tutorial/, from sources in `docs/tutorial/`.
- Add a project landing page at https://nyu-acsys.github.io/raven/, introducing Raven for users and researchers: what an IVL is and where Raven sits among Boogie, Why3 and Viper, a worked concurrent example (two threads incrementing a shared counter under an invariant, checked like every listing in the tutorial), the relationship to Iris, the CAV 2025 paper, pointers to the verified data structures and the extension API, and the team. The site root previously served the tutorial itself; it now serves this page, and the tutorial moved to `/tutorial/`.
- Add ADT ergonomics for `data` types, implemented as a new always-on extension (`lib/ext/matchExt/`, independent of `--extension`): a constructor test `x is cons`, and `match` expressions (`match xs { case nil => 0; case cons(e, t) => e }`). Constructor names are unqualified, resolved against the scrutinee's own type the same way plain destructor access (`x.elem`) already is; both forms also work on the stdlib `List[T]`. Arms are checked for exhaustiveness -- absent a trailing wildcard arm (`case _ =>`), every constructor must be named exactly once -- and a pattern variable may be written `_` to bind nothing. `is` binds at the same level as `==`, so it composes into a larger formula without parentheses (`a is cons && b is cons`).
- Extend the Extension API with a `disambiguate_expr_ext` hook, for extension constructs that bind variables of their own. Raven alpha-renames a callable body's locals and rejects unbound identifiers in a pass that runs *before* type-checking, so until now such a binder's references were rejected as unbound before `type_check_expr` ever ran. Extensions can now take over that pass for their own nodes. It has a meaningful default at the base of the chain (recurse structurally, leave the tag alone), so only binding constructs need to implement it; `match` arms above are the reference implementation. The expression grammar's `rel_expr`/`eq_expr` levels are now `%public` as well, so an extension's parser fragment can add an operator at a chosen precedence instead of only at the top of the ladder. See `docs/ext/README.md`.

### Changed

- Semicolons are no longer accepted between top-level module members (fields, procs, modules, etc.); they are now only meaningful terminating a statement in a procedure/lemma body or (optionally) separating the case branches of a data type declaration.
- Replace 'admitted' keyword with 'free' and unify handling of admitted and free symbols in AST.
- Set prophecy extension to be the default extension.
- Refactored the Extension API to remove global mutable state: the active extension is now threaded through the pipeline as an explicit value rather than mutable references.
- Overhauled user-facing error messages throughout the verifier (parser, lexer, type-checker, atomicity analysis, extensions, and the SMT backend) for clarity and consistency: vague or jargon-heavy wording was reworded, extension messages no longer leak internal implementation details (e.g. `[EXT]` tags or OCaml identifiers), internal-invariant violations are now correctly labeled as Internal Errors instead of ordinary or verification errors, solver-communication failures now point users at `--verbosity=debug`/`log.smt2`, and a handful of outright bugs turned up along the way were fixed (a crash with no installed Z3, an incomplete message, a tuple-index message missing its index, and a couple of copy-pasted/mismatched messages).
- `assert e with { ... }` moved from a parser-level rewrite into a proper extension (`AssertWithExt`, on by default regardless of `--extension`), which is what makes the type-directed fix below possible. Along the way, the Extension API gained a second, self-contained `stmt_ext` extension point for statements that need to carry a nested proof block rather than just expression arguments -- see `docs/ext/README.md`.

### Fixed

- Fix `--lsp-mode` discarding the content of every "Related Location" that pointed into an included file. Such an entry had both its position and its message replaced with a generic "originates in included file" anchored at the `include` directive, so an editor showed neither what the related location said nor where it was -- for a violated interface axiom, the two entries naming the rule and the failing assertion were lost entirely. Related locations now keep their own file, position and message, which is what lets an editor turn them into a jump to the offending declaration. Primary diagnostics are still anchored at the `include` that pulled the file in, since an LSP diagnostic is displayed in the document it is reported against, but they now keep their own message and name the file they really came from.
- Fix modules implementing an interface from the standard library, or from a file pulled in by `include`, escaping both halves of the interface-conformance check: a module could leave members of the interface entirely undefined, and any axiom the interface declared went unchecked against the module's own definitions. `module M : Library.ResourceAlgebra { rep type T = Int func comp(x: T, y: T) returns (res: T) { x } }` was accepted despite defining none of `id`, `frame`, `valid` or `fpuAllowed`, and despite a `comp` that cannot satisfy `compCommute`. The standard library, and every `include`d file, is force-marked free so it isn't re-verified for every program, but that status was recorded as `UserFree` (a `free` the user wrote) rather than `MachineFree` (established by the compiler), and it was inherited by the abstract members a user module picked up from it -- which suppressed the abstract-member check and turned every inherited axiom into an assumption. The same module written against an interface declared in the same file was rejected correctly all along. Inherited abstract members no longer arrive free, so both checks apply uniformly however the interface was reached. A `free` the user actually wrote is still honoured, so an interface may declare deliberately uninterpreted members (`free func`, `free val`, `free auto axiom`) that implementors inherit without defining; `var_is_free` now carries the full status rather than a bool so that `free val` remains distinguishable.
- Fix a variable named after a destructor field shadowing that destructor for the rest of the enclosing callable, so that a later `x.elem` failed to resolve (`Identifier elem unbound in scope`, or `Expected destructor identifier, but found variable elem`). Two defects combined: renamed binders aliased the source ident they were renamed away from, since the first freshening of a name yielded number 0 -- what every source-level ident carries -- so a `forall elem: Int` or a `match` arm's `case cons(elem, tl)` landed in scope as plain `elem`; and the disambiguation pass rewrote the field-name operand of `x.f` as though it were an ordinary variable reference, letting a local of that name capture it. Field names in `x.f` are now resolved only against `x`'s type, and generated names are numbered from 1.
- Fix bug preventing a functor to abstract over module members of its interface.
- Fixed some loop holes when type-checking modules against interfaces.
- Fix duplicated error message when a call precondition (or a desugared loop invariant) fails to verify.
- Fix rewriting of List[T] types inside abstract modules (#38).
- Reject procedure/lemma calls embedded in expressions (e.g. return statements) instead of failing later with a confusing error.
- Fix contracts of recursive (and mutually-recursive) functions failing to verify by auto-generating an inductive proof lemma for each function's ensures clause.
- Fix tuple constructors in the SMT-LIB encoding being rejected as ambiguous by newer Z3 versions.
- Fix misleading error when `import M._` (import-all) is used on an uninstantiated generic functor: report a clear diagnostic at the import site instead of a confusing "unknown identifier" error attributed to the stdlib's own source (#40).
- Explain type mismatches that are really the same underlying module reached through two different names (a manifest alias, or two separate instantiations of the same functor with the same arguments) instead of just printing two similarly-named types with no context.
- Fix SMT backend crash ("unknown sort '$tuple_n'") when a program needs a tuple wider than the previously hardcoded limit of 10 (e.g. a function with many return values, or a wide atomic-invariant token): the SMT-LIB tuple sorts declared to Z3 are now computed from the actual elaborated program instead of an arbitrary fixed range (#28).
- Fix confusing "Internal Error: no active extension recognizes this ..." when a file uses syntax from an extension that isn't active for the current run (e.g. an `eris`-only construct run without `--extension eris`, or vice versa): since the parser accepts every known extension's grammar regardless of which one is selected, this previously surfaced as an internal error rather than a normal diagnostic. The type-checker now checks whether some other known `--extension` choice would have recognized the construct and, if so, reports which one and how to select it, instead of a bare "nothing recognizes this".
- Fix a spurious "Unknown identifier `<Interface>.<callable>`" type error when a module implementing an interface inherits an atomic procedure with a body (i.e. one using `bindAU`) unchanged from the interface. The procedure's atomic-update ghost token is typed `AtomicToken<Interface.callable>` at the point the interface member is first checked; the qualified-name substitution that carries inherited members over into the implementing module had no case for the `AtomicToken` type constructor, so the token's type kept pointing at the interface's (now out-of-scope) name instead of the module's.
- Reject a callable's return variable(s) when used in its own `requires` clause -- they only come into scope in `ensures` clauses. Previously this type-checked silently, since return variables were added to scope before either clause was processed.
- Reject a direct (non-`fpu`) write to a non-ghost field from within a ghost scope (a `lemma`/`pred`/`invariant` body, or any other ghost context). Since the entire statement is erased before compilation, this write had no effect on the compiled program while still letting a proof believe the field's value changed -- previously accepted silently, since only the written value's ghost-ness was checked, not the target field's.
- Fix "Internal Error: pr_sort: unexpected sort: Bot" crash when implicit generic-functor instantiation (see `M[Int]`/`M.foo(3)` above) tries to infer a type argument from an inherently ambiguous argument, e.g. an empty collection literal like `{||}` with no other context to pin down its element type: unification was accepting `Bot` (the placeholder type such a literal gets absent any expectation) as if it were a genuinely inferred type, silently synthesizing a bogus instantiation instead of rejecting the call. Now reported as an ordinary type error at the ambiguous argument, e.g. "the type of `{||}` cannot be uniquely determined here".
- Fix an unsoundness where reassigning a local variable between an invariant's `unfold` and its matching `fold` (e.g. `unfold test(x); x := y; fold test(x);`) went undetected, letting resources escape their invariant: both places that matched an invariant instance re-evaluated the source-level argument expression at each occurrence instead of the value it denoted when the instance was opened. `unfold` now freezes its arguments into a snapshot, and the matching `fold` must prove its own arguments equal it.
- Fix `create()`-style procs -- ones that only allocate a fresh invariant instance and return it via `ensures` -- being rejected at any call site whose own contract didn't happen to also mention that invariant (#23); covered by the mask redesign above.
- Fix a spurious "Cannot assign to value ..." type error on `val x := new(...)` (with or without an explicit type annotation): the parser desugars this into a variable declaration followed by a separate allocation statement that writes into it, but unlike ordinary assignment/call/field-read initializers, the allocation statement had no way to mark itself as the variable's initializing write, so the type-checker treated it as an illegal write to a constant. `Stmt.new_desc` now carries an `is_init` flag, threaded through the same way as the other initializer forms.
- Fix an unsoundness in `assert e with { ... }` that let an *impure* fact (a permission or predicate assertion, e.g. `assert p(x) with { fold p(x); }`) be proved for free, since the construct's proof block is checked and then discarded, and nothing pays for whatever resource it produced. `e` must now type-check as `Bool`, rejecting the impure case as an ordinary type error. Also fixes a narrower, related gap: a `forall`-headed goal's bound variables could previously be reassigned inside the proof block, invalidating the generalization to "for all"; they are now `const` throughout the proof.

## [1.1.0] - 2026-01-27

### Added

- Added ExtensionAPI as a way to modularly add and remove front-end features in Raven.
- Added cmpxchg atomic operation.
- Added a new cmdline option `--extension` which (presently) takes one of three values: `default`, `eris`, and `prophecy`. This option can be used to select which extension of Raven are active for a given run.

### Changed

- Upgraded [Array interface](test/arrays/array.rav) from an interface to a module.
- Finished implementation of [rdcss](test/ext_prophecy/rdcss.rav).
- Added expr_ext_rewrite_types, stmt_ext_rewrite_types to ExtAPI.
- Added parser support for auCommit resources; enable 'helping'.
- Expanded definition of "word-sized" types to more flexible types such as algebraic sums of base types.
- Overhauled extensions to follow namespace-style convention.
- Added support for helping by allowing auPred resources to refer to another proc; overhauling parser, type-checking and rewriting.
- Updated syntax so that commitAU always requires at least two argument: token and a tuple of return values (if function returns nothing then it should be ()); and an optional third argument which is a tuple of the callable args (which goes as the middle argument). This optional argument becomes required if the token refers to a different procedure.


### Fixed

- Fixed bug in ComputeStats functionality with looking up abstract members without entering modules.
- Fixed bug regarding missing quantifiers for auto lemmas with arguments.
- Fixed two bugs in type checker related to interface aliasing (#35).
- Fixed a bug causing a crash on datatype destructors.
- Sanitizing JSON output in LSP mode.
- Catch module-level var declarations in type checker rather than parser (#31).
- Fix type-checking bug that allowed access to members of interfaces from outside of their scopes.

## [1.0.1] - 2025-11-22

### Fixed

- Supressed generating log files unless debug mode is used.

- Fixed multiple redundant local variable declarations due to excessive havocing.
- Variables declared inside while loops are now declared as local variables when while loops are rewritten to proc calls. (#29).
- Fix missing locations in certain expressions being constructed.
- Fix SMT Backend crash with "_" ident (#22).
- Fix crash on FPU stmt in while loops (#27).
- Added if_block before making call to while_loop proc.
- Fix spurious type error when type-checking inherited modules (#25).
- Fix spurious type error when assigning from data type destructor (#26).

## [1.0.0] - 2025-06-02

### Added

- Initial release of Raven.
